# What never enters the build context.
#
# Two rules, in order of how much they matter:
#
# 1. Nothing private or heavy gets baked into an image that may be pushed to a registry --
#    no `.env` with local checkpoint paths, no weights, no recordings.
# 2. Nothing that changes on every edit and is rebuilt anyway gets to invalidate a layer --
#    caches, virtualenvs, compiled bytecode.
#
# The build only needs `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock`, `packages/`, `apps/`, `scripts/` and the
# handful of files those reference. Everything below is either dangerous or dead weight.

# Local model paths and secrets. `.env` holds absolute paths to this machine's checkpoints;
# an image carrying it would resolve them inside the container, where they do not exist.
# `.env.example` is not matched by these -- it is documentation, and it is meant to ship.
.env
.env.local
**/.env
**/.env.local

# Version control and CI
.git
.gitignore
.github

# The host's virtualenv. It holds Linux wheels built against the host's Python and 5+ GB of
# CUDA libraries; the image builds its own.
.venv
venv

# Python build artefacts and caches
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Model weights. These are mounted at run time, never copied in. A single codec checkpoint is
# ~4 GB, which is most of an image on its own.
*.ckpt
*.safetensors
*.pt
*.bin
*.onnx
*.gguf

# Audio. No recording belongs in an image: they carry licensing and voice-likeness questions,
# and a generated `out.wav` sitting in the checkout would ship with every build.
*.wav
*.mp3
*.flac
*.ogg
*.opus
*.m4a

# Prose. The image serves speech, not a documentation site.
docs/
mkdocs.yml

# Run outputs
outputs/
runs/
wandb/
site/
out/

# `docker/` itself stays in the context: the image copies `docker/entrypoint.sh` out of it.
# `docker/.env` -- compose's own configuration, which names host checkpoint directories -- is
# already excluded by the `**/.env` rule above.
